# Emperor penguin

The emperor penguin is the largest living penguin and the only one that breeds
through the Antarctic winter, on fast ice far from open water. It pursues
Antarctic silverfish, squid and krill below the surface on descents that
routinely pass two hundred metres and have been recorded beyond five hundred,
lasting up to twenty minutes. Haemoglobin with a high affinity for oxygen, a
collapsible ribcage and a lowered heart rate make such an excursion survivable.
The male incubates the single egg on his feet for two months in darkness and
without food while the female returns to the sea. See [[spheniscidae]].
